1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
This is a question that sounds modern but isn't. The desire to have sex without having children is not a product

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,000
of the 20th century or the sexual revolution or the invention of the pill.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:19,000
It is one of the oldest human desires on record. And the attempts to act on that desire to find ways to prevent pregnancy

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:28,000
deliberately go back further than most people realize. Documented across cultures and time periods with a consistency that suggests this was never

5
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:35,000
a marginal concern. It was a universal one. People have always wanted to control their reproduction. The specific

6
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:42,000
methods they used ranged from the surprisingly effective to the genuinely alarming to the philosophically creative. Some of them worked, some of

7
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:51,000
them did not. And the history of what people tried, why they tried it, and how well it succeeded tells you a great deal about how ancient humans understood

8
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,000
their own bodies, their environments, and the relationship between the two.

9
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Let us start with the oldest documented evidence because it is older than most people expect. The Kahun gynecological

10
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:11,000
papyrus, an ancient Egyptian medical text dating to around 1825 BCE, contains what are almost certainly the oldest

11
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:19,000
written contraceptive prescriptions in existence. The recipes it describes include a peserie, essentially an internal barrier device made from

12
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:26,000
crocodile dung mixed with fermented dough to be inserted before sex. Another recipe uses a mixture of honey and

13
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:34,000
natron, the sodium carbonate salt used extensively in Egyptian religious and practical contexts. Crocodile dung as a

14
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:41,000
contraceptive. I want to acknowledge that before we move on. The ancient Egyptians looked at the problem of unwanted pregnancy and said, "What if we

15
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:50,000
used crocodile dung?" And the answer from a modern chemical standpoint is that it might have actually done something. Crocodile dung is alkaline

16
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,000
and alkaline environments are hostile to sperm which prefer a slightly acidic pH.

17
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:04,000
The fermented dough would have added to the effect. It was not an effective contraceptive in any reliable sense, but it was not pure magical thinking either.

18
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:13,000
It was an attempt to apply observed chemical logic to a biological problem using the materials available in a world where crocodiles were apparently

19
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:20,000
plentiful enough to provide the raw material on a recurring basis. The honey and natron mixture was similarly not

20
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:31,000
insane from a chemical standpoint. Honey has mild antimicrobial and spermicidal properties that modern research has confirmed. Natron is strongly alkaline.

21
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Neither is a reliable contraceptive, but neither is nothing. And in the context of what was available in 1825 BCE, these

22
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:49,000
represented genuine attempts at evidence-based medicine applied to reproductive control. working with the limited pharmacological toolkit of the

23
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:57,000
ancient world and trying to do something useful with it. That is more than can be said for a lot of ancient medicine.

24
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:04,000
Egypt was not alone. Ancient Mesopotamian texts contain references to contraceptive preparations.

25
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Ancient Indian texts from the Ayurvedic tradition described numerous methods for preventing conception, including oil-based preparations applied before

26
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:22,000
intercourse and various herbal formulations taken internally. Ancient Chinese medical texts contain similar material. The pattern is consistent.

27
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Wherever human civilization produced written records, those records include attempts to prevent pregnancy, which means the practice almost certainly

28
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:38,000
predates the writing by a significant margin. The question is how far back it goes. And the answer to that requires thinking about what kind of evidence

29
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:47,000
could possibly survive from before the era of written documentation. The biological evidence from actual prehistoric populations is suggestive in

30
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:56,000
an interesting way. Archaeological and skeletal studies of prehistoric populations consistently show birth spacing, the gap between births, that is

31
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:03,000
longer than would be expected if women were simply becoming pregnant at maximum biological rates. Hunter gatherer women,

32
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:11,000
both in the archaeological record and in contemporary traditional societies, tend to have children spaced roughly 3 to

33
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:16,000
four years apart rather than the theoretical maximum of 1 to two years.

34
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Some of this spacing is explained by extended breastfeeding, which suppresses ovulation through hormonal mechanisms.

35
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:31,000
Some is explained by the relatively low body fat percentages of active huntergatherer women, which also affects fertility. But some of it has been

36
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:38,000
attributed by researchers studying contemporary traditional societies to deliberate practices aimed at preventing

37
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:46,000
or spacing pregnancies. The spacing was not entirely accidental. It was at least partly managed using whatever tools and

38
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:54,000
knowledge were available. The plant world is where the most substantial and most interesting evidence lives. Across an enormous range of traditional

39
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:03,000
cultures on every inhabited continent studied by ethnobbotonists and anthropologists over the past century and a half, there exists a consistent

40
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:11,000
body of knowledge about plants with contraceptive, abortacient, or fertility affecting properties. The specific

41
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:19,000
plants vary by region because the flora of subsaharan Africa is different from the flora of North America or Southeast Asia. But the categories of plants used

42
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:26,000
and the methods of use show remarkable consistency across cultures that had no contact with each other whatsoever. This

43
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:34,000
consistency is not a coincidence. It reflects the fact that plant secondary metabolites, the chemical compounds that

44
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:42,000
plants produce for their own biological purposes, including defense against insects and fungal pathogens, include a significant number of compounds that

45
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:49,000
interact with mamalian reproductive systems. Plants did not produce these compounds for humans to use as contraceptives. They produce them for

46
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:58,000
unrelated reasons. But the interaction with mamalian hormones and reproductive biology is real and was discovered by

47
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:05,000
human populations through observation and experimentation over very long periods of time. Somebody noticed

48
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:12,000
something. They told somebody else. The knowledge accumulated generation after generation in the specific informal

49
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:15,000
channels through which traditional women's medical knowledge traveled.

50
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:23,000
Queen Anne's lace wild carrot dus karota is perhaps the most well doumented example of a plant with traditional

51
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:30,000
contraceptive use across independent cultures. Seeds of wild carrot were described by Hypocrates in the fifth

52
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:37,000
century B.CE as a contraceptive and continue to be used in traditional medicine in parts of South Asia today.

53
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:45,000
Modern research has found that wild carrot seeds contain compounds that interfere with progesterone synthesis and implantation and animal studies have

54
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:53,000
confirmed anti-fertility effects. It is not a reliable or safe contraceptive in any modern clinical sense. The dose

55
00:06:53,000 --> 00:07:02,000
required for effect is close to the dose that causes toxicity, but it is not pharmacologically inert either. Something is genuinely happening

56
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:10,000
at the biochemical level when you consume it in the right quantities which is more than can be said for many of the medical treatments deployed across the

57
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:17,000
ancient world. Sulfium is the most dramatic example of ancient contraceptive plant use and it is also

58
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:22,000
the most tragic because it was driven to extinction by the demand it created.

59
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:29,000
Sulfium was a plant that grew only in a specific region of North Africa near the ancient Greek colony of Serene in what

60
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:37,000
is now Libya. It was described extensively by ancient authors Hypocrates, Theoprastus, Dioscoridities,

61
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:44,000
Ply the Elder as having powerful contraceptive and abortacient properties. And it was prized so highly

62
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:51,000
that it became the primary export and economic foundation of sirene. Depicted on the city's coins, traded across the

63
00:07:51,000 --> 00:08:00,000
Mediterranean world at prices that made it one of the most valuable commodities of the ancient world. Worth its weight in silver in some accounts, worth more

64
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:08,000
than gold in others. The demand for sulfium driven substantially by its reputation as a contraceptive was

65
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:16,000
apparently so intense and so sustained that it was harvested to extinction by the first century CIE it was gone. The

66
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:24,000
last known stock reportedly sent to the emperor Nero as a curiosity. Ancient authors describe it with a specificity

67
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:31,000
and consistency. the dose, the method of use, the effects, the distinction between its contraceptive and

68
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:41,000
abortacient applications at different doses. That suggests the reputation was based on actual observed efficacy rather than pure superstition or marketing.

69
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:48,000
What compounds it contained and exactly how they worked is a question we cannot now answer because the plant no longer

70
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:55,000
exists. An entire species was consumed into non-existence by the demand created by its own usefulness. That is a

71
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:03,000
specific and somewhat extraordinary kind of tragedy. Penny royal, armisia species, pomegranate seeds, juniper

72
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:10,000
berries, blue kohash, wild ginger, cotton root bark across Greek, Roman,

73
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:18,000
Chinese, Indian, indigenous American and African traditional medicine. Plants in these categories appear with consistent

74
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:24,000
descriptions of fertility affecting contraceptive or abortacient properties.

75
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:32,000
The pharmacological research on many of these plants has confirmed that they contain bioactive compounds with effects on reproductive hormones, implantation

76
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:40,000
or uterine function. The efficacy and safety profiles vary enormously. Some of the traditional abortacants work partly

77
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:49,000
by poisoning the mother to a degree that also terminates the pregnancy which is as alarming as it sounds and which ancient medical authors were sometimes

78
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:56,000
genuinely aware of and documented. But the pharmacological reality underlying the traditional knowledge is real. These

79
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:05,000
plants contain things that do things to reproductive biology. The ancient Greeks and Romans were particularly systematic about documenting contraceptive

80
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:11,000
knowledge and the surviving texts from these traditions are the most detailed we have from the ancient world.

81
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:20,000
Sarannis of Ephesus, a Greek physician practicing in Rome in the 2n century CE wrote a gynecological text called gynica

82
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:28,000
that contains an extensive and remarkably organized section on contraception and abortion. His approach is notably sophisticated for the period.

83
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:36,000
He distinguishes carefully between contraceptives, substances that prevent conception, and abortations, substances that terminate

84
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:45,000
an established pregnancy, and notes that contraception is preferable to abortion both for effectiveness and for the woman's health.

85
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:53,000
He describes a hierarchy of methods organized by reliability and safety and acknowledges the trade-offs between efficacy and safety in a way that

86
00:10:53,000 --> 00:11:02,000
reflects genuine clinical thinking rather than simple pharmarmacological optimism. His recommended methods include barrier approaches, wool

87
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:14,000
peseries soaked in various substances including olive oil, cedar resin, white lead, and pomegranate pulp and oral preparations taken before or after sex.

88
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:26,000
Some of his recommendations have obvious pharmacological logic that survives modern scrutiny. Olive oil and cedar resin both have spermicidal properties confirmed by modern research.

89
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:36,000
Pomegranate seeds contain phytoistrogens, plant compounds that mimic estrogen that affect the hormonal environment in ways relevant to fertility.

90
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:45,000
White lead is genuinely effective at killing things, including sperm. Though, it also kills the woman over time through lead poisoning, which represents

91
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:52,000
a significant downside that Sarannis apparently considered an acceptable trade-off under certain circumstances.

92
00:11:52,000 --> 00:12:01,000
Ancient medicine was not always characterized by what we might call a thorough long-term risk assessment. The Roman poet Oid, writing in the 1st

93
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:08,000
century B.C.E., mentions contraception as a common practice among the women of Rome, treating it as unremarkable and

94
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:16,000
widely known rather than as a specialty medical procedure. This casual reference is itself evidence. When a poet treats

95
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:24,000
something as unremarkable background detail rather than notable subject matter, it suggests the practice was common enough that the audience required

96
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:31,000
no explanation. The Talmud, the central text of rabbitic Jewish law compiled over several centuries, contains

97
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:39,000
extensive discussion of contraception and its permissibility in various circumstances, including specific mention of a contraceptive device called

98
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:46,000
a mock, a material inserted before intercourse to prevent conception, and oral contraceptive preparations

99
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:55,000
referred to as a cup of roots. The discussion is legal and philosophical rather than purely medical. But the underlying assumption that effective

100
00:12:55,000 --> 00:13:03,000
contraception exists and that the legal question is when it is permissible to use it tells you that the methods being discussed were understood as actually

101
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:11,000
working to some meaningful degree. You do not write lengthy legal argument about the conditions under which something is permitted if that something

102
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:19,000
reliably does nothing. Physical barrier methods appear across multiple ancient traditions in ways that represent genuine engineering solutions to the

103
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:28,000
same mechanical problem. In addition to the Egyptian pesery recipes and the Greek and Roman wool peseries, there are references in various ancient texts to

104
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:37,000
something functionally equivalent to a condom, coverings for the male genitals used during intercourse. Ancient Egyptians appear to have used linen

105
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:44,000
sheets, possibly for disease prevention as much as contraception, though the two functions are not mutually exclusive.

106
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:52,000
There is evidence from ancient Japanese traditions of similar coverings made from tortoise shell and later from animal intestine, which sounds deeply

107
00:13:52,000 --> 00:14:01,000
uncomfortable in ways that go some way toward explaining why barrier contraception has historically been underutilized relative to its theoretical effectiveness.

108
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:11,000
Ancient Rome saw the use of goat bladder for the same purpose. The principle was understood across multiple independent traditions even if the specific execution varied considerably.

109
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:18,000
Withdrawal kitus interrupt us appears so universally across traditional cultures and historical periods that it requires

110
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:25,000
no particular documentation of specific ancient sources. It is mentioned in the Hebrew Bible in the story of Onan from

111
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:33,000
which it derives one of its historical names. It is described by ancient medical authors as a common practice. It

112
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:41,000
is documented in virtually every traditional society ever studied across any part of the world. Its effectiveness as a contraceptive is real but

113
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:50,000
imperfect. Modern estimates suggest it reduces pregnancy risk by roughly 73 to 96% depending on consistent and correct

114
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:57,000
use, which is a range that tells you everything about how consistent and correct use varies in practice. As a

115
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:09,000
freely available, cost-free, no preparation required method, its appeal across essentially the entire span of human history is self-explanatory.

116
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:16,000
Lactational amenorhea, the suppression of ovulation by extended breastfeeding, was probably the most widely used and

117
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:24,000
most effective ancient contraceptive method. Though calling it a method implies a level of deliberate strategic deployment that may not always have been

118
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:32,000
present in the way modern people would understand it, extended breastfeeding suppresses the hormonal signals that trigger ovulation. And in populations

119
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:40,000
where breastfeeding was intensive and prolonged, which it was in most traditional societies, often until the child was 2 or 3 years old, this effect

120
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:44,000
produced meaningful birth spacing without any additional intervention.

121
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:52,000
Whether ancient populations understood the hormonal mechanism or simply observed that extended nursing tended to delay the next pregnancy is unclear, but

122
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:59,000
the practical observation would have been available to any attentive person living within the community over time, and it would have been correct. Some

123
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:07,000
traditional societies appear to have deliberately extended breastfeeding at least partly for its contraceptive effect rather than purely for

124
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:14,000
nutritional reasons. The anthropological evidence from contemporary traditional societies includes explicit statements

125
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:22,000
from women about the contraceptive motivation for extended nursing. Whether this understanding is ancient or a more recent accumulation of community

126
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:30,000
observation is impossible to determine with certainty. But the effect itself is as old as human breastfeeding. And the

127
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:39,000
observation of the effect is probably nearly as old. Abstinence and sexual restriction during specific periods of the menstrual cycle appear in various

128
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:44,000
ancient medical and religious frameworks as a mechanism for controlling reproduction.

129
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:53,000
The concept of a fertile window, the period during which conception is possible, was understood to exist across ancient medical traditions, even if its

130
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:56,000
actual timing was generally misidentified.

131
00:16:56,000 --> 00:17:05,000
Hypocrates believed conception was most likely immediately after menration, which is approximately the least fertile period of the average cycle. This meant

132
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:14,000
that ancient attempts at rhythm method style abstinence were often practiced at precisely the wrong time, providing little or no actual contraceptive

133
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:21,000
benefit while creating a false sense of protection. The actual timing of ovulation, midcycle, roughly 2 weeks

134
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:27,000
before the next menstrual period, was not correctly understood until the 20th century.

135
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:35,000
For the entire span of recorded history before roughly 1930, anyone trying to use cycle timing as a contraceptive was

136
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:42,000
working from information that was not just incomplete, but actively incorrect in its most important detail. The social

137
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:50,000
and economic dimensions of reproductive control in ancient societies deserve more attention than they typically receive because the motivation for

138
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:58,000
avoiding pregnancy was not simply personal preference. It was embedded in complex social structures that made childbearing differently weighted for

139
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:07,000
different people in different circumstances. In ancient Rome, slave women had the most obvious and most urgent need for contraceptive knowledge.

140
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:16,000
Pregnancy for an enslaved woman meant a child born into slavery. And the social reality of sexual violence against enslaved people made unwanted pregnancy

141
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:24,000
a constant threat rather than a hypothetical one. Free women faced different but still significant pressures. Marriage was partly an

142
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:31,000
economic and political alliance in which the production of legitimate heirs was a primary social expectation.

143
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:38,000
Having too many children could strain household resources. Having children in complicated circumstances, adultery was

144
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:47,000
a legal offense with severe consequences under certain periods of Roman law made reproductive control a practical necessity rather than a preference.

145
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:55,000
Across cultures, the women most likely to have access to contraceptive knowledge and the most pressing reasons to use it consistently were often those

146
00:18:55,000 --> 00:19:02,000
working in the sex trade. The heterai of ancient Greece, the women working in the Roman brothel that were legal,

147
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:10,000
regulated, and taxed institutions of Roman urban life. These women had the most consistent motivation to seek out

148
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:18,000
contraceptive methods and the most persistent need for knowledge that actually worked. Some researchers have argued that the preservation and

149
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:26,000
transmission of contraceptive knowledge across ancient cultures owes more to this specific population than to formal medical traditions precisely because the

150
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:32,000
practical need was so immediate and so consequential. What is the overall picture that emerges from all of this?

151
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:41,000
Ancient humans were trying very hard to control their reproduction. They were doing so across every culture in every time period for which we have evidence

152
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:49,000
using the plant knowledge and physical materials and conceptual frameworks available to them. The effectiveness of what they were doing varied enormously

153
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:57,000
from the meaningfully effective lactational amenorhea and withdrawal to the moderately effective spermicidal barrier methods to the pharmacologically

154
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:05,000
real but unreliable herbal preparations to the pure placebo of amulets and rituals. The knowledge was primarily

155
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:12,000
held and transmitted by women, often informally passed between mothers and daughters and between women in the same

156
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:20,000
community. It was not written down in the same way that elite male medical knowledge was written down, which means it is substantially under represented in

157
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:28,000
the historical record despite being more widely practiced than what the surviving texts suggest. Serenus of Ephesus wrote

158
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:35,000
things down. the grandmother who told her granddaughter which plants to drink a tea from and when did not. And in

159
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:43,000
terms of what people were actually doing in practice, the grandmother's knowledge was almost certainly more widely distributed and more regularly applied.

160
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:52,000
The gap between what people wanted, reliable reproductive control, and what they actually had access to. A patchwork of partially effective methods with

161
00:20:52,000 --> 00:21:01,000
significant uncertainty about which worked and why was a gap that persisted through essentially all of human history and was only seriously closed by the

162
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:05,000
development of modern hormonal contraception in the midentth century.

163
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:13,000
But the desire that drove the development was not new. It was not created by modernity or the sexual revolution. It was there in the

164
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:22,000
crocodile dung recipes of ancient Egypt and the sulfium trade of the ancient Mediterranean and the herbal knowledge passed between women in every

165
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:30,000
traditional society ever studied on every inhabited continent. The desire to have sex without having children and the

166
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:39,000
willingness to take considerable risks and deploy whatever resources were available to pursue that desire is as old as the desire to have sex itself,

167
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:42,000
which given everything is about as old as it gets.
